  <section id="monday" aria-labelledby="monday-title">
    <h2 id="monday-title">What to do Monday</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Find every place a service changes its database and then sends a message. Each is a dual write until the message is an outbox row in the same transaction.</li>
      <li>Add a per-aggregate version and increment it with <code>UPDATE ... RETURNING</code> in the same transaction that writes the outbox row. Do not read it first and add one in the application.</li>
      <li>Decide the relay. If it is Debezium, capture the outbox table only, apply the router only to it, and write down which version of the reference you read.</li>
      <li>Put the aggregate id in the key and the version where the consumer can read it (a header or the envelope), and check what a plain consumer actually receives.</li>
      <li>Make every consumer apply an event only if its version is higher than the last one applied for that aggregate, and keep deletes as markers.</li>
      <li>If a consumer keeps seen event ids, prune them by time, longer than your longest rewind, never by offset.</li>
      <li>Choose the cleanup: delete in the same transaction (log tailing only), a partition you drop, or delete after publish plus a safety window. Never <code>UPDATE</code> outbox rows when the router is in use.</li>
      <li>Run the six faults in the testing table against staging, and keep the failing case you find as a regression test.</li>
      <li>Mark each &ldquo;check your version&rdquo; item above with an owner and a test.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="verified" aria-labelledby="verified-title">
    <h2 id="verified-title">What was run, and what was not</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Read on 2026-10-09:</strong> the Debezium Outbox Event Router reference (stable pages, which are 3.7, and the 1.9, 2.7, 3.0 and 3.3 versions for option names), the Debezium blog post on the outbox pattern, the Debezium PostgreSQL connector page (&ldquo;Behavior when things go wrong&rdquo;), the Debezium exactly-once page, microservices.io (the transactional outbox, polling publisher and transaction log tailing pages), the PostgreSQL pages on logical decoding, output plugins, transaction isolation and partitioning, and the Kafka introduction, producer configuration and operations pages. The router&rsquo;s <code>apply()</code> method was read in the 3.0.0.Final source.</li>
      <li><strong>Run:</strong> the producer SQL (the two transactions, a rolled-back transaction, a global sequence consumed by a rollback, the polling cycle with a repeated <code>SELECT</code>, and the insert-then-delete transaction) on PGlite, which embeds PostgreSQL. The consumer guard and the four arrival sequences in the table ran on PGlite and on SQLite; the SQLite run is part of this repository&rsquo;s tests.</li>
      <li><strong>Not run:</strong> Debezium, Kafka Connect and Kafka, so the connector configuration, the routed message layout (including a header arriving as bytes) and the router&rsquo;s behaviour on an update or on a tombstone are from the documentation and the 3.0.0.Final source only. Two concurrent database sessions: PGlite has one, so the lock-wait behaviour and the interleaving in the consumer section are from the PostgreSQL documentation and my reasoning. The effect of an immediately-deleted outbox on slot loss is my reasoning. No managed database service was used.</li>
      <li><strong>Check your version:</strong> router option names and defaults, the <code>partition</code> placement, whether tombstones are dropped, producer defaults in your Kafka Connect worker, and the isolation level your application uses.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

